WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.459
Lesson three.

00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:07.478
How do you climb a landscape you can't see, when all you can do is stand somewhere and measure the height?

00:00:07.478 --> 00:00:13.110
You throw a cloud of guesses, keep the best ones, and learn the shape of the cloud as you go.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.287
Here's a landscape in two dimensions.

00:00:16.287 --> 00:00:19.322
Green is better, and the cross marks the top.

00:00:19.322 --> 00:00:22.657
We start with one guess, the mean, in orange.

00:00:23.257 --> 00:00:26.871
First, sample a cloud of candidates around the mean.

00:00:26.871 --> 00:00:30.845
The size of the cloud is the step size, called sigma.

00:00:31.445 --> 00:00:37.466
Then evaluate every candidate, rank them, and keep the best half, shown in green.

00:00:38.066 --> 00:00:43.259
Move the mean to a weighted average of the winners, with the best ones counting most.

00:00:43.259 --> 00:00:44.934
That's one generation.

00:00:44.934 --> 00:00:46.072
Repeat.

00:00:46.672 --> 00:00:49.867
Only the ranking matters, not the exact scores.

00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:53.352
One terrible score can't drag the search off course.

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:55.801
That makes the method robust.

00:00:56.401 --> 00:01:00.422
Now two versions side by side, on a long, tilted valley.

00:01:00.422 --> 00:01:04.569
On the right, a plain strategy, with a fixed, round cloud.

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:06.628
On the left, CMA-ES.

00:01:07.228 --> 00:01:11.919
Watch the left cloud stretch and turn until it lines up with the valley.

00:01:11.919 --> 00:01:18.552
The plain one keeps sampling a circle, wastes most of its guesses on the valley walls, and zig zags.

00:01:19.152 --> 00:01:21.614
CMA-ES also adapts its step size.

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:24.677
It keeps a running sum of its recent moves.

00:01:24.677 --> 00:01:29.120
If they point the same way, it's on a long slope, and sigma grows.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.924
If they cancel out, it's circling around a peak, and sigma shrinks.

00:01:34.524 --> 00:01:44.202
On this plot, the error of CMA-ES drops by more than thirty orders of magnitude, while the plain version gets stuck near the bottom of the valley.

00:01:44.802 --> 00:01:47.320
The C in CMA stands for covariance.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.428
It's the shape of the cloud.

00:01:49.428 --> 00:01:51.945
In two dimensions, it's an ellipse.

00:01:51.945 --> 00:01:58.686
For the walker, it's a table with almost nine hundred thousand numbers, one for every pair of weights.

00:01:59.286 --> 00:02:02.429
It learns which weights should move together.

00:02:02.429 --> 00:02:09.251
If raising a hip weight only helps when a knee weight rises too, CMA-ES starts proposing both at once.

00:02:09.851 --> 00:02:12.560
The number of candidates matters too.

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:18.040
On a bumpy landscape full of small traps, a small population can get caught in one.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.578
A bigger population sees more of the landscape at once and finds the real peak.

00:02:24.178 --> 00:02:27.435
For the walker, the whole loop is a few lines.

00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:31.235
Each generation asks for forty eight candidate walkers.

00:02:31.235 --> 00:02:34.734
Every candidate walks the same five random tracks.

00:02:34.734 --> 00:02:41.066
Then we hand back the scores, and CMA-ES updates its mean, its step size and its covariance.

00:02:41.666 --> 00:02:43.217
Why not gradients?

00:02:43.217 --> 00:02:47.690
Methods like PPO and TD3 estimate a gradient from individual steps.

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:51.805
They use fewer samples, but they need many more moving parts.

00:02:51.805 --> 00:02:57.590
An evolution strategy only looks at whole episodes, which is simple and runs in parallel.

00:02:57.590 --> 00:02:59.319
The price is samples.

00:02:59.319 --> 00:03:04.092
Next, in lesson four: what happens when every measurement is noisy.
